---
source: hh/tests/cli_conformance.rs
expression: "String::from_utf8_lossy(&out.stdout)"
---
Export a session as a JSON bundle, a portable `.hh` archive, or a self-contained HTML page.

Exports are ALWAYS redacted by default — nothing leaves the machine unredacted by accident. Opting out (--no-redact) requires an interactive confirmation.

Usage: hh export [OPTIONS] [SESSION]

Arguments:
  [SESSION]
          Session short id, full id, or `last`. Defaults to `last`

Options:
  -o, --out <OUT>
          Write to this file instead of stdout (`-o -` is stdout explicitly)

      --html
          Export a self-contained HTML page instead of the JSON bundle

      --open
          With `--html`: open the page in your default browser. Without `-o`, the page is written to `<data_dir>/exports/`. Only honoured when stdout is a terminal and `CI` is unset; never fails the command

      --no-open
          Do not open a browser (overrides an earlier `--open`, e.g. from an alias)

      --bundle
          Export a portable `.hh` archive (manifest + events + referenced blobs, zstd-compressed) instead of the plain JSON bundle. Import it elsewhere with `hh import`

      --no-redact
          Skip redaction (requires interactive confirmation; refused when stdin is not a TTY, so a script can never exfiltrate raw sessions)

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

Examples:
  hh export last --out session.json           redacted JSON bundle to a file
  hh export last --bundle -o session.hh       portable archive for `hh import`
  hh export a1b2c3 --html --out session.html  self-contained HTML replay page
  hh export last --html --open                HTML page under the data dir, opened in your browser
  hh export last | jq .session                JSON bundle to stdout
